  "content": {
    "id": "p33-q7-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "7",
    "prompt": "Let $f:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R$ be a function.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p33-q7-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that $f_{\\mathrm{even}}(x)=\\frac12(f(x)+f(-x))$ is an even function.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f_{\\mathrm{even}}(-x)=\\frac12(f(-x)+f(x))=f_{\\mathrm{even}}(x)$, so $f_{\\mathrm{even}}$ is even.",
          "worked": "For every $x\\in\\mathbb R$,\n$$\\begin{align*}f_{\\mathrm{even}}(-x)&=\\frac12\\bigl(f(-x)+f(-(-x))\\bigr)\\\\f_{\\mathrm{even}}(-x)&=\\frac12\\bigl(f(x)+f(-x)\\bigr)\\\\f_{\\mathrm{even}}(-x)&=f_{\\mathrm{even}}(x).\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore, $f_{\\mathrm{even}}$ is even.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 35
      },
      {
        "id": "p33-q7-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Show that $f_{\\mathrm{odd}}(x)=\\frac12(f(x)-f(-x))$ is an odd function.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f_{\\mathrm{odd}}(-x)=-\\frac12(f(x)-f(-x))=-f_{\\mathrm{odd}}(x)$, so $f_{\\mathrm{odd}}$ is odd.",
          "worked": "For every $x\\in\\mathbb R$,\n$$\\begin{align*}f_{\\mathrm{odd}}(-x)&=\\frac12\\bigl(f(-x)-f(-(-x))\\bigr)\\\\f_{\\mathrm{odd}}(-x)&=-\\frac12\\bigl(f(x)-f(-x)\\bigr)\\\\f_{\\mathrm{odd}}(-x)&=-f_{\\mathrm{odd}}(x).\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore, $f_{\\mathrm{odd}}$ is odd.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 35
      },
      {
        "id": "p33-q7-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Show that every function $f:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R$ can be uniquely decomposed into the sum of an even function and an odd function.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p33-q7-c-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "f(x)=f_{\\mathrm{even}}(x)+f_{\\mathrm{odd}}(x).",
                    "display": false
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p33-q7-c-short-reason",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Any two decompositions differ by a function that is both even and odd, hence identically zero; the decomposition is unique.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "By parts (a) and (b), $f_{\\mathrm{even}}$ is even and $f_{\\mathrm{odd}}$ is odd. For every $x\\in\\mathbb R$,\n$$\\begin{align*}f_{\\mathrm{even}}(x)+f_{\\mathrm{odd}}(x)&=\\frac12\\bigl(f(x)+f(-x)\\bigr)+\\frac12\\bigl(f(x)-f(-x)\\bigr)\\\\f_{\\mathrm{even}}(x)+f_{\\mathrm{odd}}(x)&=f(x).\\end{align*}$$\nThus a decomposition exists.\n\nSuppose also $f(x)=E(x)+O(x)$, where $E$ is even and $O$ is odd. Then\n$$f_{\\mathrm{even}}(x)-E(x)=O(x)-f_{\\mathrm{odd}}(x).$$\nThe left side is even and the right side is odd. Denote their common function by $h$. Since $h$ is both even and odd,\n$$\\begin{align*}h(x)&=h(-x)\\\\h(x)&=-h(x).\\end{align*}$$\nHence $h(x)=0$ for every $x\\in\\mathbb R$. Therefore $E(x)=f_{\\mathrm{even}}(x)$ and $O(x)=f_{\\mathrm{odd}}(x)$, proving uniqueness.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 80
      }
    ]
  },
